{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CfbeNjRZE4syeX77UytD3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# How a Bicycle Rim Brake Works

When you squeeze the brake lever on the handlebars, you pull a cable that runs along the frame toward the brake mechanism. This cable is under tension and transmits your hand's force mechanically to the brake assembly. The harder you squeeze the lever, the more the cable tightens, creating greater mechanical advantage and stopping power.

The cable pulls the brake arms together, forcing the brake pads against the rim of the wheel. These rubber pads create friction against the metal rim surface, which slows the wheel's rotation. The friction converts the wheel's kinetic energy into heat, gradually bringing your bicycle to a stop. Releasing the lever allows a spring to push the brake arms apart, separating the pads from the rim."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":64,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":166,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null}

